            <!-- B) Quick Definition -->
            <section id="def" class="card mb-3">
                <div class="card-body">
                    <h2 class="h5">Quick Definition</h2>
                    <p class="mb-2">
                        A <b>web application</b> is a software system where the user interface is delivered through web pages,
                        and the core processing runs on a server. The browser requests pages/data, and the server responds with HTML/JSON/files.
                    </p>

                    <div class="alert alert-info mb-0">
                        <b>Real-life analogy:</b><br />
                        Think “food delivery app”:
                        <ul class="mb-0">
                            <li>Your browser/app screen = UI (what you see and click)</li>
                            <li>Backend server = logic (cart totals, delivery rules, offers, payments)</li>
                            <li>Database = stored data (users, orders, restaurants)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- C) Why it matters -->
            <section id="why" class="card mb-3">
                <div class="card-body">
                    <h2 class="h5">Why Web Applications Matter (Industry View)</h2>
                    <ul class="mb-2">
                        <li><b>Zero-install for users:</b> Works on any device with a browser.</li>
                        <li><b>Single deployment point:</b> Update on server once; all users get the latest.</li>
                        <li><b>Central security control:</b> Auth, roles, logging, rate limits, and monitoring are managed centrally.</li>
                        <li><b>Integration-ready:</b> Connect with databases, payment gateways, email/SMS, third-party APIs.</li>
                        <li><b>Scale-out options:</b> Load balancing and cloud hosting help handle more traffic.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-0">
                        <b>Warning:</b> Because web apps are exposed over the internet, input validation and authentication are not optional—they are mandatory.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- D) Core Concept Explanation -->
            <section id="concept" class="card mb-3">
                <div class="card-body">
                    <h2 class="h5">Core Concept: Client–Server + Request–Response</h2>
                    <p class="mb-3">
                        Web apps run on a <b>client–server</b> model. The browser (client) sends an HTTP request.
                        The server processes it (code + data) and returns an HTTP response (HTML/JSON).
                    </p>

                    <h3 class="h6 mb-2">Diagram 1: Concept flow (Client–Server)</h3>
                    <pre class="diagram">
+------------------+         HTTP Request          +------------------------+
| Client (Browser) | ----------------------------> | Server (Web App)       |
| UI: HTML/CSS/JS  |                               | Logic: C#/Java/etc.    |
+------------------+                               +------------------------+
          ^                                                 |
          |                 HTTP Response                    |
          +-------------------------------------------------+
                       HTML / JSON / File
                    </pre>

                    <h3 class="h6 mt-3 mb-2">Diagram 2: Real request/response flow (login example)</h3>
                    <pre class="diagram">
User opens: https://site.com/Account/Login

1) GET  /Account/Login
   - Server returns Login page (HTML)

2) User submits form
   POST /Account/Login (username, password)

3) Server validates (DB), creates session/cookie, returns:
   - Redirect 302 to /Dashboard

4) Browser requests /Dashboard (GET)
   - Server returns personalized dashboard HTML
                    </pre>

                    <div class="img-placeholder mt-3">
                        <b>[Image: HTTP lifecycle]</b><br />
                        GET page → POST form → Redirect → GET dashboard
                    </div>

                    <hr class="my-3" />

                    <h3 class="h6 mb-2">Static Website vs Web Application</h3>
                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table table-bordered table-sm align-middle">
                            <thead class="table-light">
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Feature</th>
                                    <th>Static Website</th>
                                    <th>Web Application</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Content</td>
                                    <td>Mostly fixed pages</td>
                                    <td>Dynamic (changes per user/action)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Login / Roles</td>
                                    <td>Rare</td>
                                    <td>Common (Admin/User/Manager)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Database</td>
                                    <td>Optional / minimal</td>
                                    <td>Usually required</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Examples</td>
                                    <td>Portfolio, brochure site</td>
                                    <td>HR portal, e-commerce, LMS, banking</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="alert alert-success mb-0">
                        <b>Tip:</b> ASP.NET MVC is one of the structured ways to build web applications on the .NET platform.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- E) Step-by-Step -->
            <section id="steps" class="card mb-3">
                <div class="card-body">
                    <h2 class="h5">Step-by-Step: How a Web Page is Served</h2>
                    <ol class="mb-2">
                        <li>User opens a URL (example: <code>/Home/Index</code>).</li>
                        <li>Browser sends an HTTP request to the server (GET).</li>
                        <li>Server executes application code (security checks, business rules, data fetch).</li>
                        <li>Server returns an HTTP response (HTML page or JSON data).</li>
                        <li>Browser renders UI (HTML) and applies styles/scripts (CSS/JS).</li>
                    </ol>

                    <div class="alert alert-secondary mb-0">
                        <b>Corporate tip:</b> In production, step 3 also includes logging, exception handling, and performance considerations (caching, DB query tuning).
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- F) Working Example -->
            <section id="example" class="card mb-3">
                <div class="card-body">
                    <h2 class="h5">Working Example: “Hello Web App” (ASP.NET MVC style)</h2>
                    <p class="mb-3">
                        This tiny example shows the classic flow: URL → Controller → View → HTML Response.
                        You will create a full project in later topics—this is a concept demo.
                    </p>

                    <h3 class="h6 mb-2">1) Controller: <code>Controllers/HomeController.cs</code></h3>
                    <pre class="codeblock"><code>using System.Web.Mvc;

namespace MvcDemo.Controllers
{
    public class HomeController : Controller
    {
        // GET: /Home/Hello?name=Ajay
        public ActionResult Hello(string name)
        {
            name = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(name) ? "Guest" : name.Trim();

            ViewBag.Message = "Hello " + name + "! This page is served from a Web Application.";
            return View();
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

                    <h3 class="h6 mt-3 mb-2">2) View: <code>Views/Home/Hello.cshtml</code></h3>
                    <pre class="codeblock"><code>@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Hello";
}

&lt;h2&gt;@ViewBag.Message&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="alert alert-light border"&gt;
    Server executed C# code and returned this HTML to the browser.
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;a class="btn btn-primary" href="/Home/Hello?name=Ravi"&gt;Try Another Name&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

                    <h3 class="h6 mt-3 mb-2">Expected Output</h3>
                    <div class="img-placeholder">
                        <b>[Image: Browser output screenshot]</b><br />
                        Hello Ajay! This page is served from a Web Application.<br />
                        (Plus a button to try another name)
                    </div>

                    <div class="alert alert-info mt-3 mb-0">
                        <b>Try it:</b>
                        <ul class="mb-0">
                            <li><code>/Home/Hello</code> → shows Guest</li>
                            <li><code>/Home/Hello?name=Ajay</code></li>
                            <li><code>/Home/Hello?name=  Kiran  </code> (trim demo)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

